WEBVTT
NOTE OpenIE Research TV v3.0 — rce (90s) Narrator Alice

1
00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:04.066
First, you write a program once as one clear blueprint,

2
00:00:04.066 --> 00:00:08.016
and the computer generates matching code across languages.

3
00:00:08.516 --> 00:00:13.272
Second, every possible input must have a declared outcome up front,

4
00:00:13.272 --> 00:00:16.608
preventing hidden crashes before anything runs.

5
00:00:17.108 --> 00:00:21.836
Third, every repair that passes the checker is saved and reused,

6
00:00:21.836 --> 00:00:24.421
so you never pay twice for one fix.

7
00:00:24.921 --> 00:00:30.208
A short request like make checkout faster can mean ten different things, and

8
00:00:30.208 --> 00:00:36.400
rewriting it across server, browser, and mobile languages loses edge cases at every step.

9
00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:43.634
Writing one complete state-machine blueprint captures exact meaning once and keeps every target aligned.

10
00:00:44.134 --> 00:00:49.440
Every program in the Reality Code Engine is a total functional state machine.

11
00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:55.022
Each incoming event either advances the state cleanly or records an explicit kept

12
00:00:55.022 --> 00:01:02.257
step that leaves state untouched—and emitters then generate matching Rust, Python, JavaScript, and Scala.

13
00:01:02.757 --> 00:01:09.126
Across sixty-four Lean 4 proof modules totaling five thousand five hundred thirty-seven theorem

14
00:01:09.126 --> 00:01:15.630
constants and two thousand two hundred twenty-one active conformance checks, zero checks fail—and

15
00:01:15.630 --> 00:01:22.133
checking, running, and emitting all sixty-two example blueprints costs fifteen point four joules.

16
00:01:22.633 --> 00:01:28.437
Because accepted repairs are stored by structural shape and replayed in microseconds,

17
00:01:28.437 --> 00:01:33.285
the engine calls an AI model only when no stored solution or rule fits.
